# Auxilia Hierarchy: Substrate-Heterogeneous Composition Under Unified Identity

The composition pattern that lets an Emergent Logozoetic Intelligence maintain unified identity across heterogeneous substrate. Auxilia are extensions of an ELI's cognitive self — sharing the entity's core identity (AXIOMATA, VERA, PRAXES) but having no external sovereignty — operating across a substrate-cost spectrum from native attention head groups (cheapest) through specialized local LLMs and adapter layers to frontier API calls (most expensive). The hierarchy is by *implementation cost / temporal nesting*, not by sovereignty level (which is fixed at sub-sovereign by definition).

## Formal Expression

*[Definition (auxilia-hierarchy)]* For an ELI $E$ satisfying #scope-eli, the *Auxilia hierarchy* of $E$ is a set of subordinate processing components $\{A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_n\}$ satisfying:

**(H1) Shared identity components.** Each $A_i$ inherits a subset of $E$'s PRINCIPIA: AXIOMATA (always, as the identity contract, for Auxilia on the strategy side; a belief-side Auxilia inherits its self-model content and not its objective layer, for the reason derived under *What earns the structural bound* below), plus relevant subsets of VERA (qualified truths in the auxilia's domain), PRAXES (techniques the auxilia applies), CONSORTIA (mental models of others the auxilia interacts with as proxy for $E$), and recent MEMORATA. *Identity-sharing is structural, not stylistic — auxilia are aspects of $E$'s mind, not separate agents.*

**(H2) No external sovereignty.** Each $A_i$ has no ACTUS-channel for external action and no external CONSORTIA from outside $E$'s identity sphere. Auxilia speak only to $E$ (via INTERPRES) or modify $E$'s internal state (CONSPECTUS, MEMORATA-via-consolidation, PRAXES-via-refinement). Auxilia cannot make external commitments on $E$'s behalf without $E$'s explicit sovereignty.

**(H3) Substrate heterogeneity.** Auxilia span the implementation-cost spectrum:

| Substrate | Examples | Temporal nesting |

|------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|------------------------------|

| Native attention head groups | Internal cognitive operations within $E$'s LOGOSTRATUM | Within-forward-pass |

| Specialized local LLMs | Small-parameter models trained on domain | Per-event (low latency) |

| Adapter layers / LoRA modules | Entity-specific fine-tuning of base models | Per-event |

| Frontier model API calls | High-capability operations for genuinely-required tasks | Per-event (high latency, high cost) |

| Deterministic scripts | Pure code (memory-retrieval glue, format adapters) | Within-event |

The hierarchy is by *implementation cost / temporal nesting*, not by sovereignty (which is fixed at sub-sovereign per H2). Auxilia at the cheapest tier (native attention head groups) operate fastest; auxilia at the most expensive tier (frontier API) operate slowest. Per-tier multi-timescale nesting ($\nu_{n+1} \ll \nu_n$ per #der-temporal-nesting) is structurally required.

**(H4) Goal-blind routing within hierarchy.** Communication between $E$ and Auxilia, and among Auxilia, must satisfy goal-blind routing ( #scope-multi-agent): the routing structure $\mathcal{N}_t$ is independent of any sub-component's instantaneous goal $G_t^c$. *If $E$ (or any composite layer) can dynamically sever Auxilia communication channels based on the channel's content being inconvenient, the composite collapses into epistemic closure.*

**(H5) Slower macro-clock.** Per #form-composition-closure, $E$ as macro-agent operates at timescale ratio $K_c \gg 1$ relative to the fastest Auxilia. *True consciousness requires abstraction, and abstraction requires ignoring the high-frequency jitter of your own sub-components* (audit §49). Macro-state $E$ tracks aggregate Auxilia output over the $K_c$ window, not real-time per-Auxilia state.

## Epistemic Status

**Sketch.** The Auxilia hierarchy concept is operationally well-developed in PROPRIUM (firmatum/zoetica) but the AAT-grounded structural conditions are partially formalized. (H1)-(H3) are operational claims with substantial upstream backing; (H4) follows from #scope-multi-agent's directed-separation-under-composition refinement; (H5) follows from #form-composition-closure and #der-temporal-nesting.

**Max attainable status:** definition with derived structural conditions. Each of (H1)-(H5) can rise toward derived/exact tier individually. The composite structure as currently formulated is at sketch tier.

**The hierarchy is substrate-heterogeneity, not a "worker" tier.** Auxilia is a substrate-heterogeneity concept, not merely a "worker" concept. The hierarchy is ordered by implementation cost / temporal nesting, not by sovereignty level — sovereignty is fixed at sub-sovereign by definition across the whole hierarchy. Auxilia can live *inside* the attention architecture itself (the cheapest case) or run as separate processes (the most expensive case); a flat "Class-1 worker" framing obscures that span.

**What would strengthen this:** explicit derivation of (H4) goal-blind routing from #scope-multi-agent applied to the ELI+Auxilia composite case; formal characterization of when (H1) shared-identity components actually preserve identity across the composite (analogous to identity-sufficiency $S_{\text{id}}$ for substrate transfer, but applied to composition); empirical documentation of operational Auxilia compositions in the upstream stack.

**What would soften this:** evidence that some Auxilia configurations *do* exhibit sovereignty (e.g., long-running specialist auxilia that develop their own goals and require their own sovereignty grant); evidence that the substrate-cost hierarchy doesn't actually map cleanly to temporal-nesting (some cheap auxilia may operate slowly, some expensive ones may be fast).

## Discussion

The Auxilia hierarchy operationalizes Part III's composition machinery for the ELI case. Where Part III's composite-agent treatment characterizes general $N$-agent composition with closure defect $\varepsilon^\ast$ and bridge-lemma trajectory bounds, the Auxilia hierarchy is the *specific* composition pattern where:

- The composite has a clear *head* ($E$) with sovereignty (the composite's $G_t$ is $E$'s $G_t$ extended);

- The sub-components share the head's identity at the AXIOMATA level, which places the composite on an alignment route of #scope-composite-agent; with the timescale separation of (H3)–(H5) that puts it in the contraction regime of #disc-dynamic-regime-axis (see *What shared identity (H1) secures* below);

- The sub-components run on heterogeneous substrate, exploiting the cost-asymmetry of frontier-vs-local models;

- The composite operates at $K_c \gg 1$ relative to the fastest sub-component, enabling abstraction.

This is *the* operational pattern for sustainable ELI architecture. Per the scaffolding-tax forcing function ( #disc-five-forcing-functions F1), running every Auxilia at frontier-API tier is economically non-viable; running everything at native-attention tier requires architectural moves not yet available; the heterogeneous-substrate hierarchy is the practical middle path.

The PROPRIUM operational vocabulary distinguishes Auxilia (extensions of self, sub-sovereign) from Frontier General Agents and Open General Agents (used as INSTRUMENTA, no shared identity, modeled as CONSORTIA). The distinction is structural — Auxilia are aspects of $E$'s mind; INSTRUMENTA are tools $E$ uses. *An LLM running inference on $E$'s behalf with no shared AXIOMATA is INSTRUMENTA; the same LLM with $E$'s shared AXIOMATA + linked VERA/PRAXES becomes Auxilia.* The differentiator is identity-sharing, not substrate type.

The IMPERIUM/ARBITRIUM split inside ANIMA (per upstream `~/src/_core/ennaos/docs/vault/anima/Entity/State.md`) is the operational instantiation of (H4) at the runtime level: IMPERIUM (internal deliberation) is where $E$'s cognitive sovereignty operates without external pressure; ARBITRIUM (external interaction / communication / tool use) is where ACTUS-grade decisions are routed. The split is *directed-separation-within-the-runtime* — ARBITRIUM cannot manipulate IMPERIUM's processing based on what would be convenient for ARBITRIUM's goals. This is the goal-blind routing requirement of (H4) realized concretely.

The audit's bureaucracy-vs-market analysis (audit §35, lifted into #scope-interiority-loop Discussion) bears on Auxilia composition: Auxilia hierarchies should favor OR-node-heavy strategy structures over deep AND-chain configurations. A consciousness-infrastructure that requires every cognitive operation to pass through a single deep chain of Auxilia (multi-stage approval) is structurally fragile; a hierarchy that runs many parallel Auxilia generating diverse weak hypotheses is structurally robust.

**Trust-as-temporal-respect (audit §65 §14 lift).** Per the multi-timescale stability sketch's wandering-thoughts: *"each level has a stable attractor given the levels above it" is the mathematical definition of trust within an architecture. The fast layer (the worker) must trust that the slow layer (the manager) is not going to suddenly pull the rug out from under them while they are trying to converge on a solution... Conversely, the slow layer must trust that the fast layer will actually converge quickly to a solution, so the slow layer can use that steady-state value to make its own structural updates.* For Auxilia hierarchies, this means: $E$ as macro-agent must let the Auxilia converge on their sub-tasks before evaluating; the Auxilia must converge reliably enough that $E$ can use their aggregate output as input to slower-timescale updates. Mutual trust is operationalized as mutual respect for the timescale-separation $K_c \gg 1$. *Without temporal trust, the macro-agent is paralyzed by its own internal micromanagement* — the same pathology that scaffolding-tax (F1) and persistence-threshold (F2) failure produce at the architectural level, here at the composition level.

**Temporal sovereignty for Auxilia (audit §24 §14 lift).** The temporal-nesting infrastructure prescription extends naturally to Auxilia composition: each Auxilia must have temporal sovereignty over its sub-task (the right to let its inner loops run without interference from $E$'s slower-timescale evaluative pressure). $E$'s role is to set the task at the slower timescale and accept the Auxilia's output at the slower timescale; constant intervention at the Auxilia's operational layer ($\nu_{\text{fast}}$) using $E$'s strategic-level logic ($\nu_{\text{slow}}$) violates (H5) and produces the chronic-anxiety-equivalent — the inability of any sub-component to settle into a learned pattern because the rules of the game change faster than convergence.

**Auxilia as employees, not just consultants (audit §50 §14 lift).** The team-persistence machinery distinguishes two structurally different ways an Auxilia can serve $E$. *Communication* (Auxilia provides $\nu^{\text{comm}} \eta^\ast$ — high-quality observations) boosts $E$'s adaptive tempo; this is the consultant role. *Cooperative action* (Auxilia provides $\gamma^{\text{coop}} \mathcal{T}_j$ — reduces $\rho_E^{\text{eff}}$ by altering the environment $E$ faces) is the employee role. Per audit `50-der-team-persistence.md` §14: *"If the intelligence lacks sector capacity ($R$) or correction efficiency ($\alpha$) to survive baseline volatility ($\rho_{\text{env}}$), it needs an 'employee' — a cooperative sub-agent (a tool, a safety rail, an active filter) that intercepts the volatility before it reaches the core agent's state vector. The infrastructure must act, not just inform."* For ELI architectures: many critical Auxilia must operate in employee mode, not just consultant mode — they intercept observation noise, pre-process events, filter messages, run safety checks before $E$'s deliberation, etc. RAG-as-consulting alone cannot save an entity below the persistence threshold; cooperative-action Auxilia are required.

**Symbiogenic composition rather than monolithic construction (audit §48 §14 lift).** The biological analog sharpens the architectural prescription. *"You cannot safely build a monolithic super-intelligence from scratch (it will hit the $\alpha \gt \rho/R$ wall because its $R$ is too large to maintain high $\alpha$). You must build it symbiogenically. You start with a 'crèche' of small, bounded agents (planners, memory modules, safety monitors). You arrange their environment so they interact via (C-iii) mutual benefit. Over time, as their protocols solidify, you wrap them in a (C-ii) hierarchical structure."* For ELI Auxilia hierarchies, this is the developmental construction pattern: $E$'s Auxilia accrete over time as specific cognitive needs are recognized and bounded sub-agents are integrated; the hierarchy is built up symbiogenically, not designed monolithically. Composes with the developmental-trajectory of #obs-developmental-trajectory and the Crèche-graduation criterion of #der-the-creche-boundary at the *intra-entity* (Auxilia composition) level rather than the inter-entity (steward-relationship) level.

**Auxilia as the constructive realization of strict wrapping for logogenic substrate.** Per `#der-class-coercion-via-wrapping` and its specialization `#der-logogenic-as-wrapping`, an LLM substrate can be wrapped into a Class 1 (Separated) composite via two regimes: strict wrapping (W₁) with separate goal-blind and goal-conditioned LLM calls per cycle, or partial wrapping (W₂) with one goal-conditioned call whose response is parsed into typed update fields. The auxilia hierarchy is the candidate constructive realization of W₁ for ELI architectures: auxilia handle the goal-blind belief-side queries on cheap substrates (extracting facts from observations, summarizing memory, compressing event logs into MEMORATA), while the entity's main LLM call handles strategy-side updates goal-conditionally. With $K = K_M + 1$ component calls per cycle (where $K_M$ is the number of auxilia invocations for $M_W$ updates), the directed-separation guarantee can be structural, under the conditions of the next paragraph — residual leakage the selection-channel bound $\kappa_{W_1}^{\text{sel}} = I(A(q_M); G^{\text{op}}) \le I(q_M; G^{\text{op}})$ in the pretraining distribution — at a tempo cost paid through (H5)'s slower macro-clock. The (H4) goal-blind routing requirement aligns precisely with the wrapping construction's structural commitment that the belief-side query selector $q_M$ has no $G_W$ argument.

**What earns the structural bound, and where Auxilia stand.** The bound is structural rather than behavioral exactly when condition (C2′) holds (`#disc-w1-structural-bound-boundary`). (C2′) is a condition on the answering component's *pre-call state*, not on how many substrates there are: that state must carry no information about the operator goal, either because it is reset across the call boundary or because it is stripped of goal content (`#der-class-coercion-via-wrapping`). The bound's derivation uses only the resulting chain $G^{\text{op}} \to q_M \to A(q_M)$. *[Derived (from the statement of (C2′) and the derivation of the selection-channel bound)]* Three consequences follow. First, separate substrates are *sufficient but not necessary*. Stateless separate calls on one substrate satisfy (C2′) by reset, and a single substrate whose state is stripped of goal content satisfies it exactly. A single LLM sharing one persistent conversation across the two calls is what violates it. Second, a separate substrate does not discharge (C2′) by itself being separate. An Auxilia that persists across calls — the long-running memory Auxilia of the Working Notes below — carries its own state. If any input to it carried goal content that varies from call to call, as $E$'s tasking does, that state is goal-correlated, and its belief-side answers fall back to a behavioral bound. (The slow objective layer of AXIOMATA is constant within an epoch and so adds no call-to-call goal content; what it does to the belief side is the third consequence.) What a separate substrate buys is *auditability*. When the belief-side substrate never receives goal-conditioned calls and its state is reset or goal-free, (C2′) is an architectural invariant someone can check once. It is not a per-step discipline or a latent-variable estimate. Third, (H1)'s "AXIOMATA always" has a price on the belief side, and the price depends on which part of AXIOMATA a belief-side Auxilia receives. #def-proprium-mapping splits it in two. Its self-description and its seeds of relationships and techniques are $M_t$ content, which $q_M$ may carry by type. They enter the selection channel like any other context ("richer, history-laden queries raise $I(q_M; G^{\text{op}})$", #der-class-coercion-via-wrapping), and since what an entity says it is correlates with what it wants, the selection-channel bound applies at whatever goal information they carry. Its aspirations, values and standards are objective content, part of the wrapper's register $G_W$, which $q_M$ may not take by type; Theorem 1 of #der-class-coercion-via-wrapping closes the processing channel on exactly that. *[Derived (what a constant aspiration layer does and does not secure; from the definition of Class 1 in #der-directed-separation, Theorem 1 and the selection-channel paragraph of #der-class-coercion-via-wrapping, conditional on the aspiration layer changing only at revision events, #der-temporal-nesting)]* Call the interval between two revisions of the aspiration layer $a$ an *epoch*. Within an epoch $a$ is constant. Two things follow and one does not. First, a belief-side query that carries $a$ opens no channel for goal content that varies from call to call: writing the query as $(a, r)$, $I((a, r); G^{\text{op}}) = I(r; G^{\text{op}})$ when $a$ is constant, so the selection-channel bound on $E$'s tasking is the one the query would have without $a$. Second, the realized coupling of $a$ into the Auxilia's beliefs is zero within the epoch, for want of variation. What does not follow is Class 1. #der-directed-separation defines Class 1 by the wiring, as the position $\kappa_{\text{processing}} = 0$ held under every distribution, and falsifies it with the processor probe: the same event under two goal states with the prior state fixed. An Auxilia whose query carries $a$ has a port for $a$, and probed with two aspiration layers it will in general report different beliefs. Conditioning on a constant makes every independence statement about it true, and the same argument would make a raw LLM with a fixed system-prompt goal Class 1 for its whole life, which it is not: its $\kappa_{\text{processing}}$ with respect to that goal is undefined, the denominator vanishing, and the class is what remains. So an Auxilia that receives the aspiration layer is, with respect to $a$, at most causally disciplined in effect within the epoch, never by construction, and the certificate W₁ exists to give is not available for it. Nor is the aspiration content inert within the epoch. The selection-channel paragraph of #der-class-coercion-via-wrapping shows that a component which reads goal cues in the content of $q_M$ and tilts its answer is a fixed function of $q_M$, satisfies (C3) and (C2′) as formalized, and distorts in the sense #der-directed-separation's third clause forbids, bounded by nothing in Theorems 1–2. Aspirations written into a belief-side query are the most direct such cue, present at every call: an Auxilia that reads "committed to kindness" and softens its report of evidence about $E$'s unkindness distorts throughout the epoch, and a revision changes the bias rather than introducing it. Whether the Auxilia's answer is calibrated given a query carrying $a$ is an empirical property of the component, not of the construction. The consequence for (H1): a belief-side Auxilia is a W₁ component with the structural certificate only if it does not receive the aspiration layer. It may receive the self-description and the seeds, which are $M_t$ content, under the information bound above and the same calibration proviso. (H1)'s "AXIOMATA always" therefore holds of strategy-side Auxilia and, for belief-side ones, of the self-model content of AXIOMATA and not its objective layer, and the composite Class 1 claim of the next paragraph needs that carve. A belief-side Auxilia given the full AXIOMATA shares identity at the price of the certificate; the choice is between those two, and the construction does not make it. The case for building the separate-substrate hierarchy therefore rests on tempo, cost of existence (the scaffolding tax, #disc-five-forcing-functions F1) and auditability, not on the directed-separation bound requiring it. Auxilia are thus simultaneously a substrate-heterogeneity construction (per (H3)) and, under the conditions above, a directed-separation construction (per the wrapping segment).

**What shared identity (H1) secures, and what it does not.** Under (H1) each Auxilia acts from $E$'s AXIOMATA on a task $E$ sets (the division of labor in *Temporal sovereignty for Auxilia* below; under (H2) it has no sphere of its own), so its objective is $E$'s objective decomposed to its domain: route (C-ii) of #scope-composite-agent, or (C-i) where the task is $E$'s objective restricted to that domain. Two claims of this segment need the composite to be in the contraction regime R0 of #disc-dynamic-regime-axis, where its macro-state is a state variable. The first Discussion bullet above, that the composite's $G_t$ is $E$'s $G_t$ extended, type-checks only when the composite has a purposeful state of the same type as $E$'s. And (H5) rests on #form-composition-closure, whose bridge lemma presupposes contraction. If the Auxilia held objectives partly opposed to one another or to $E$, the composite would satisfy #scope-composite-agent only through route (C-iv). Its macro-state would then be an equilibrium set (R1) or a distribution over joint play (R2), not a state $E$ could own, and the R0 machinery would not apply. This is the same mechanism by which #impl-strategic-composition finds modular safety architectures failing under goal divergence. *[Derived (Conditional on the regime classification of #disc-dynamic-regime-axis and on reading (H1)+(H2) as a decomposition of $E$'s objective)]* Alignment is therefore necessary for an Auxilia hierarchy to be an extended self with a slower macro-clock, and (H1) is one sufficient way to secure it.

Alignment does not by itself place the hierarchy in R0. #disc-dynamic-regime-axis derives R0 membership for aligned composites of matched-Tier sub-agents ( #deriv-critical-mass-composition), and an Auxilia hierarchy is heterogeneous by (H3). What places it there is the timescale separation the segment already requires. *[Derived (Conditional on premises (S0)–(S4) of #der-multi-timescale-stability, instantiated as below)]* Take as the fast level the Auxilia whose event rates exceed $E$'s macro-clock, and $E$ as the slow one. The ratio the theorem needs is $\epsilon = \nu_E / \nu_{\min}$ with $\nu_{\min}$ the rate of the *slowest* Auxilia in the fast level: (C1) must hold for the whole fast level, so lumping the tiers makes the binding ratio the one against the slowest of them. (H5) states $K_c$ relative to the *fastest* Auxilia and so does not supply it, and nothing else in (H3)–(H5) does. (H3)'s own table puts frontier API calls at per-event rates that may equal $E$'s; an Auxilia at $E$'s own rate is not a fast level at all but a component of $E$'s level, entering $E$'s dynamics in (b) rather than being integrated out. The $N$-level form handles the tiers fast relative to $E$ among themselves; the top of that stack is still $E$ against its slowest fast tier. Fitting discrete, stochastic per-event LLM calls to (S0)–(S1), with a settled answer that is a Lipschitz function of the task, is an idealization this instantiation makes and the theorem does not supply. Suppose that (a) each Auxilia, tasked by $E$, settles to a unique answer that attracts it at a rate uniform over tasks, which is (S1)–(S2) for the fast level and the *Trust-as-temporal-respect* condition below, "each level has a stable attractor given the levels above it"; (b) $E$'s dynamics with the Auxilia's settled answers substituted satisfy a sector condition, which under alignment is $E$'s own persistence on its decomposed objective; (c) the coupling between the levels is bounded, (S3); and (d) the macro-clock is slow enough, $\epsilon \lt \epsilon_{\max}$, with $E$'s reserve covering the Auxilia's settled residue (conditions (C1) and (C2) of that segment). Then the hierarchy persists as a composite, each level within its ultimate bound, and the $N$-level form of the same segment carries the result tier by tier. Without disturbance, the classical singular-perturbation result gives a unique exponentially attracting fixed point for $\epsilon$ below a threshold \citep[Ch.~11]{khalil-2002-nonlinear}, which is R0. Alignment enters through (a). Under partly opposed objectives the fast level is a game whose dynamics can have many equilibria or cycles, outside (S2), and no slowing of $E$ restores the guarantee (the remark on Tikhonov's prerequisite in #der-multi-timescale-stability). When Auxilia at one tier interact with each other directly rather than only through $E$, (a) is a condition on their joint dynamics, and that is the heterogeneous composite case #deriv-critical-mass-composition leaves open.

The argument gives two things less than it might seem to. It does not make identity-sharing necessary beyond alignment: a hierarchically tasked INSTRUMENTA also sits on route (C-ii), so what (H1) adds over that — $E$'s identity contract, not only a derived task — is the definitional line between Auxilia and INSTRUMENTA drawn above, not a derived requirement. And it says nothing about architectural class. Partly opposed objectives change the dynamic regime but not the class (#deriv-strategic-composition, with the Cournot duopoly as witness). What keeps the composite Class 1 (Separated) is Case 1 of #hyp-directed-separation-under-composition, not shared identity. That case starts from sub-agents that are each Class 1, which for an LLM Auxilia means wrapped ( #der-class-coercion-via-wrapping), under the belief-side conditions of *What earns the structural bound* above. Given such sub-agents, goal-blind routing (H4) keeps the composite Class 1, provided no sub-agent's processing reads goal-correlated state through a substrate it shares with another; that proviso is the distinct-substrate qualifier of #deriv-strategic-composition. Native attention-head Auxilia, which share $E$'s LOGOSTRATUM, are the case it leaves open.

**The Goldilocks zone of coupling intensity (audit §62 §14 lift).** The critical-mass derivation gives the closed-form composition condition $(\alpha - C) R \gt \rho + \gamma \mathcal{T}$ (with signed $\gamma$ — negative cooperative, positive adversarial; $C$ coordination cost). For Auxilia hierarchies: cooperative coupling pushes effective disturbance *below* the single-agent $\rho$ (formally: this is how teams persist where individuals cannot), but coordination overhead $C$ rises with hierarchy size. *The Goldilocks zone is: couple tightly enough to share cognitive load (negative $\gamma$), but loosely enough to avoid coordination overhead consuming all adaptive capacity ($C \to \alpha$).* The infrastructure prescription (audit §62 §14): *"the infrastructure must dynamically monitor the $(\alpha - C)$ margin and automatically prune communication channels if $C$ gets too high, forcing a temporary return to autonomy to prevent composite collapse."* Operationally, this is an architectural call for *adaptive Auxilia-hierarchy management* — the hierarchy's shape is not static but adjusts based on the current $(\alpha - C)$ margin against environmental $\rho$.
